[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE`: Rust exited 0 where the C was killed by signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose reader had already closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | termination |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | empty | empty | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`), shell status 141 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | empty | empty | exit code 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduction:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printf '5\n' | c_src/build/driver | true              # ${PIPESTATUS[1]} == 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printf '5\n' | translation/target/release/driver | true # was 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` in its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pre-`main` runtime setup. A C program inherits the default disposition, so its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `write` (during the implicit `stdout` flush at exit) is turned into a fatal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signal. In Rust the same `write` instead returned `EPIPE`, which `main` discarded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m along with every other write error, leaving a normal exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is exactly the class of bug the task description warns about: stdout and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr were byte-identical, so a stdout-only assertion passed while the exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status was wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default handler as the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m thing `main` does, via a direct `signal(2)` call:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn reset_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `sigpipe_termination_matches`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that already matched, and were pinned with tests
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where the C does something surprising. Each was confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the real binary rather than assumed, and each now has a test so a future
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m edit cannot silently drift.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` reads across newlines.** `%d` skips *all* leading whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`' '`, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`), so `"\n\n\n42"` yields 42. An `fgets`-style
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation would stop at the first newline and print 300 instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `scanf_reads_across_newlines`, `whitespace_only_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **The return value of `scanf` is ignored.** On matching failure (`"abc"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"-"`, `".5"`) and on EOF (empty input), `x` keeps its initializer `0` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program prints `300` and exits 0. There is no error path and nothing is ever
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   written to stderr. → `empty_input`, `matching_failure_paths`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A lone sign is a matching failure, not a zero.** `"-"`, `"+"`, `"- 5"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"--5"` all print 300. → `explicit_signs`, `matching_failure_paths`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Values wider than `int` are truncated, not rejected.** glibc converts `%d`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   through a `long` and stores the low 32 bits, so `4294967296` → `0` → prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `300`, and `4294967301` → `5` → prints `310`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `values_beyond_int_are_truncated`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Values beyond `long` saturate, then truncate.** glibc clamps to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` and stores the low 32 bits, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `99999999999999999999999` → `-1` → prints `298`, while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-99999999999999999999999` → `0` → prints `300`. The Rust `scanf_d` reproduces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   this with an `i64` accumulator plus a saturation flag, then `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `values_beyond_long_saturate`, `very_long_digit_runs` (2000- and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   10000-digit runs).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`2*x + 300` wraps.** Signed overflow is UB in C but the emitted code wraps,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so `1073741674` prints `-2147483648`. The Rust uses `wrapping_mul` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `wrapping_add`; note that plain `*`/`+` would panic in a debug build, so this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   matters for `cargo test` as well as `cargo build --release`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `arithmetic_overflow_wraps`, `int_boundaries`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%d` is strictly base 10.** `"010"` is ten, not eight; `"0x1f"` converts as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0` and leaves `x1f` unread. → `leading_zeros_are_decimal`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Only the first number is consumed.** `"1 2 3"` prints `302`; the unread tail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is simply discarded at exit, even when it is a megabyte long.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `only_first_number_is_read`, `large_unread_tail`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **NUL bytes are ordinary non-digit bytes.** They neither terminate nor count as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whitespace, so `"\0" + "5"` is a matching failure. → `embedded_nul_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printf("%d\n")` has no padding and exactly one trailing newline**, for every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   magnitude and for negative results. → `formatting_across_magnitudes`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `output_sign_transitions` (`-150` → `0`, `-151` → `-2`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argv` is never read**, so extra arguments change nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `arguments_are_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Unreadable stdin behaves like EOF.** Closed fd 0, `/dev/null`, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   directory on stdin all print `300` and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `stdin_closed`, `stdin_from_dev_null`, `stdin_is_a_directory`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A failed stdout write that is not a broken pipe is swallowed.** With stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   on `/dev/full` the flush fails with `ENOSPC` and the C still exits 0 with empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stderr; the Rust must not report the error. → `stdout_write_error_is_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage beyond the enumerated cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
